// `std:utf8` — the boundary between VL's strings and the byte world.
//
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. A VL `string` is TODAY a sequence of Unicode CODE
// POINTS: `s.length` counts code points, `s[i]` and `s.charCodeAt(i)` yield the
// whole code point, and `fromCodePoints` builds one back from an `i32[]`. A
// `u8[]` is a sequence of BYTES — `u8` is a STORAGE type, so an element is one
// byte that reads out widened to `i32` (0..255) and truncates on write, the same
// split wasm itself makes between i8 as an array element and i8 as a value,
// which does not exist. Files, paths, argv, stdin and every other boundary VL
// will grow are bytes; UTF-8 is the conversion between the two worlds, and it
// has to be written exactly once. It lives in its own module rather than
// privately inside `std:fs` because `std:fs` is merely the FIRST consumer, and a
// private copy is how a program ends up with two decoders holding two different
// opinions about invalid input.
//
// THE WORD "TODAY" IN THAT PARAGRAPH IS LOAD-BEARING, and the whole audit of
// this module is `docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md`. `strings-design.md`
// §Storage makes a string's backing UTF-8 BYTES; §API makes `s[i]` a byte and
// `.length` a byte count; §Codepoints moves code points to `for cp in s` /
// `s.cpAt(i)` / `s.cpLen()`. When that lands, the two worlds this module
// converts between STOP BEING TWO WORLDS at the level of storage, and the
// module's job shrinks to the part that is not storage:
//
//   `utf8Length(s)`  becomes  `s.length`      — an O(n) count becomes O(1)
//   `encodeUtf8(s)`  becomes  `s.bytes()`     — an O(n) transcode becomes an
//                                               O(1) zero-copy view (§Byte view)
//   `decodeUtf8`/`decodeUtf8At`/`decodeUtf8Lossy` and `Utf8Error` STAY, because
//   §Validity is deliberately Go-lean — the CORE does no validation at the host
//   boundary and lets malformed bytes decode leniently to U+FFFD. VALIDATION,
//   POSITIONED ERROR REPORTING AND SANITIZATION ARE THIS MODULE'S REMAINING JOB,
//   and there is nothing in the core that will ever do them.
//
// The two exports that collapse are also the only two that read a `string`, and
// they are written by ITERATION rather than by INDEX for exactly that reason —
// see `encodeUtf8`. Everything else here takes a `u8[]`, which is already bytes
// and therefore does not move at all.
//
// THE INVALID-INPUT RULING, both directions, because it is the whole design:
//
//   ENCODE IS TOTAL. `encodeUtf8` cannot fail. A VL string is an i32 sequence
//   and nothing stops it holding -1, 0x110000 or a lone surrogate
//   (`fromCodePoints` accepts any `i32[]`) — none of which is a Unicode scalar
//   value, and none of which has a UTF-8 encoding. Each such element is encoded
//   as U+FFFD. The alternative — a fallible encoder — would infect the signature
//   of every caller that merely wants to name a FILE, for an input no correct
//   program produces, and it would still have to emit *some* bytes. Substitution
//   buys the invariant that matters: what leaves here is always well-formed
//   UTF-8, so the host that receives it (a Rust `str`, a POSIX path) never
//   rejects it.
//
//   DECODE IS FALLIBLE, AND STRICT BY DEFAULT. `decodeUtf8` returns
//   `string | Utf8Error` and rejects every ill-formed sequence: bad lead byte,
//   bad continuation, overlong form, surrogate, beyond U+10FFFF, truncated
//   tail. This is Rust's `from_utf8`, not `from_utf8_lossy`, and the reason the
//   strict one is the DEFAULT is that a decode failure is information: those
//   bytes are not text, and the program about to treat them as text is about to
//   be wrong. Silently substituting U+FFFD would turn "this is a PNG" into a
//   string of question marks that compares unequal to everything and explains
//   nothing. `decodeUtf8Lossy` is the deliberate opt-out for a caller who would
//   rather render mojibake than stop — a log tailer, an editor opening whatever
//   it was handed — and substitutes one U+FFFD per maximal ill-formed subpart,
//   which is the WHATWG rule.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO. No UTF-16, no other encodings, no BOM handling
// (a leading U+FEFF decodes to U+FEFF and stays in the string — stripping it is
// a policy for whoever knows the format), no normalization, no grapheme or
// display-width analysis. Those are not "later here"; they are a different
// module.

// A decode failure, carrying the position AND the offending byte.
//
// `at` is a BYTE OFFSET — RULED, not merely inherited; see
// `docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md` §at. It is the offset of the failure
// WITHIN THE DECODED RANGE — relative to the `off` that was passed in, never an
// absolute index — so it stays meaningful to a caller decoding a slice out of a
// larger array. That is Rust's `Utf8Error::valid_up_to()`, which is likewise
// relative to the slice handed to `from_utf8`. `byte` is the lead byte that
// could not be decoded, which was previously only stringified into `msg` and so
// could not be inspected without parsing prose.
//
// BYTES IS THE ANSWER AFTER §API TOO, AND THE REASON IS THAT IT WAS NEVER
// ANYTHING ELSE. The input is a `u8[]`; `at` counts positions in a byte array,
// so there is no code-point reading of it to lose. What CHANGES is that it stops
// being an offset in a foreign coordinate system and becomes one in the ONLY
// coordinate system (§API: byte offsets are what `slice` takes, what `indexOf`
// returns, and what `std:regex` will report) — so `s.slice(0, e.at)` is the
// valid prefix, with no converter. It also acquires a second reading it does not
// have today: because UTF-8 storage makes decode a byte identity, `at` is
// simultaneously the byte LENGTH of the successfully decoded prefix. Today those
// two numbers differ for any non-ASCII prefix. Pinned in
// `tests/cases/std/utf8-invariant.vl` §6 as `at == encodeUtf8(prefix).length`,
// which is true on both sides of the swap.
//
// `byte` IS ALSO WHAT KEEPS THIS TYPE DISTINCT. `error-handling-design.md` §90
// blesses `ParseError = { at: i32, msg: string }`, and VL aliases are
// STRUCTURAL — so the two-field shape would make `Utf8Error` and `ParseError`
// literally one type, and a union naming both (`Config | ParseError | Utf8Error`,
// which that document's own worked example is one step away from) would fail at
// emit with `union box atom test on a union with no recorded members`.
// `ParseError` does not exist in code yet, so this module would have silently
// taken its seat and the first parser VL grows would have collided.
export type Utf8Error = { at: i32, byte: i32, msg: string }

// U+FFFD, substituted in both directions.
const REPLACEMENT = 0xFFFD

// `cp` if it is a Unicode scalar value, U+FFFD otherwise. The three non-scalars
// an i32 can hold: below the range, above it, and the surrogate block
// D800..DFFF — which UTF-8 must never carry (a codec that accepts them is
// CESU-8/WTF-8, not UTF-8).
//
// THE SWAP TAKES THIS RULING AWAY FROM THIS MODULE AND HANDS IT TO THE CORE, and
// that hand-over is an item on `utf8-byte-ready.md`'s checklist rather than a
// detail. `fromCodePoints` stores a non-scalar VERBATIM today — measured:
// `fromCodePoints([0xD800]).charCodeAt(0)` is 55296, `[0x110000]` is 1114112,
// `[-1]` is -1 — which is only possible because the backing is `i32`. Once the
// backing is UTF-8 bytes there is no such storage, so `fromCodePoints` itself
// must substitute, trap, or drop. It matters that it picks SUBSTITUTE: `print`
// already DROPS non-scalars (`print(fromCodePoints([97, 0xD800, 98]))` writes
// `ab`) while `encodeUtf8` substitutes U+FFFD, so the tree already holds the two
// different opinions about invalid input this module's header exists to prevent
// — and the swap is the moment one of them has to win.
function scalar(cp: i32) {
  if cp < 0 { return REPLACEMENT }
  if cp > 0x10FFFF { return REPLACEMENT }
  if cp >= 0xD800 && cp <= 0xDFFF { return REPLACEMENT }
  cp
}

// How many bytes `cp` occupies once encoded — applying the SAME substitution the
// encoder applies, which is what makes `utf8Length` an exact prediction of what
// `encodeUtf8` produces rather than an estimate. One width function, so the
// measurement and the encoder cannot drift.
function utf8Width(cp: i32) {
  const c = scalar(cp)
  if c < 0x80 { return 1 }
  if c < 0x800 { return 2 }
  if c < 0x10000 { return 3 }
  4
}

// `s.utf8Length()` — the exact length of `s.encodeUtf8()`, WITHOUT encoding it.
// Distinct from `s.length`, which counts code points TODAY and bytes after the
// §Storage swap: the two agree for ASCII and for nothing else, and the direction
// of the disagreement is about to flip. Exported because it is the only way to
// answer "how big will this be on the wire" — a size check, a quota, a header
// field — short of producing the bytes and throwing them away.
//
// **It iterates, it does not index** — see `encodeUtf8` for why, and
// `docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md` for the fact that this export collapses to
// `self.length` the day the swap lands.
export function utf8Length(self: string) {
  let n = 0
  for cp in self { n = n + utf8Width(cp) }
  n
}

// `s.encodeUtf8()` — `s` as UTF-8 bytes. Total: a non-scalar element encodes as
// U+FFFD (this module's header).
//
// THE LOOP IS `for cp in self`, NOT `while i < self.length`, AND THAT IS LOAD-
// BEARING. The body is an ENCODER: every branch of it reads its input as a CODE
// POINT and writes BYTES. `for cp in self` yields code points today and
// `strings-design.md` §Codepoints keeps it yielding code points after `s[i]` and
// `.length` become byte-valued — so producer and consumer are a matched pair
// whose meanings move together. The indexed spelling agreed only by coincidence:
// the day `charCodeAt` reads a byte, `"é"` (C3 A9) would encode as C3 83 C2 A9,
// which is the double-encoding defect `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md`
// §Builders found in `std:str`. Same class, opposite direction, no type error to
// catch it — a byte and a code point are both `i32`.
export function encodeUtf8(self: string): u8[] {
  const out: u8[] = []
  for cp in self {
    const c = scalar(cp)
    if c < 0x80 {
      out.push(c)
    } else if c < 0x800 {
      out.push(0xC0 | (c >> 6))
      out.push(0x80 | (c & 0x3F))
    } else if c < 0x10000 {
      out.push(0xE0 | (c >> 12))
      out.push(0x80 | ((c >> 6) & 0x3F))
      out.push(0x80 | (c & 0x3F))
    } else {
      out.push(0xF0 | (c >> 18))
      out.push(0x80 | ((c >> 12) & 0x3F))
      out.push(0x80 | ((c >> 6) & 0x3F))
      out.push(0x80 | (c & 0x3F))
    }
  }
  out
}

// The one decode loop. The strict/lossy fork is a PARAMETER rather than two
// copies so the two public spellings cannot disagree about what "ill-formed"
// means — the bug that shows up as a validator and a consumer accepting
// different byte strings.
//
// The validity table is the standard one, and every row of it rejects a sequence
// a naive shift-and-or would happily decode:
//   C0 C1        overlong two-byte forms of ASCII
//   E0 80..9F    overlong three-byte forms
//   ED A0..BF    the surrogate block
//   F0 80..8F    overlong four-byte forms
//   F4 90..BF    beyond U+10FFFF
//   F5..FF       no such lead byte
// Accepting any of them is the classic security bug: two byte strings decoding
// to one string is how a path check that already ran gets walked past.
function decodeCore(
  self: u8[],
  off: i32,
  len: i32,
  lossy: boolean,
): string | Utf8Error {
  const cps: i32[] = []
  let i = 0
  while i < len {
    const b0 = self[off + i]
    // The sequence length comes from the lead byte, together with the TIGHTENED
    // bounds the FIRST continuation byte must satisfy (every later one is the
    // plain 0x80..0xBF).
    let size = 0
    let cp = 0
    let lo = 0x80
    let hi = 0xBF
    if b0 < 0x80 {
      size = 1
      cp = b0
    } else if b0 >= 0xC2 && b0 <= 0xDF {
      size = 2
      cp = b0 & 0x1F
    } else if b0 >= 0xE0 && b0 <= 0xEF {
      size = 3
      cp = b0 & 0x0F
      if b0 == 0xE0 { lo = 0xA0 }
      if b0 == 0xED { hi = 0x9F }
    } else if b0 >= 0xF0 && b0 <= 0xF4 {
      size = 4
      cp = b0 & 0x07
      if b0 == 0xF0 { lo = 0x90 }
      if b0 == 0xF4 { hi = 0x8F }
    }
    // `good` counts the MAXIMAL VALID SUBPART — how far the sequence got before
    // it went wrong, never less than 1. It is what the lossy path advances by,
    // so a truncated 4-byte sequence at the end of the range yields ONE U+FFFD
    // rather than one per leftover byte. That is the WHATWG rule, and it is also
    // the only advance that cannot loop: `good >= 1` always.
    let ok = size > 0
    let good = 1
    let k = 1
    while ok && k < size {
      if i + k >= len {
        ok = false
      } else {
        const b = self[off + i + k]
        let blo = 0x80
        let bhi = 0xBF
        if k == 1 {
          blo = lo
          bhi = hi
        }
        if b < blo || b > bhi {
          ok = false
        } else {
          cp = (cp << 6) | (b & 0x3F)
          good = good + 1
        }
      }
      k = k + 1
    }
    if ok {
      cps.push(cp)
      i = i + size
    } else if lossy {
      cps.push(REPLACEMENT)
      i = i + good
    } else {
      return {
        at: i,
        byte: b0,
        msg: "invalid UTF-8: byte " + toString(b0) + " at offset " + toString(i),
      }
    }
  }
  fromCodePoints(cps)
}

// `b.decodeUtf8()` — the whole array as a string, or the position of the first
// ill-formed sequence. STRICT; see the header for why strict is the default.
export function decodeUtf8(self: u8[]): string | Utf8Error {
  decodeCore(self, 0, self.length, false)
}

// `b.decodeUtf8At(off, len)` — the same, over one range. The range form exists
// because a consumer that splits a byte block into records (`std:fs`'s
// directory listing, a line reader) must attribute a decode failure to the
// RECORD, not to the block.
export function decodeUtf8At(
  self: u8[],
  off: i32,
  len: i32,
): string | Utf8Error {
  decodeCore(self, off, len, false)
}

// `b.decodeUtf8Lossy()` — the whole array, with one U+FFFD per maximal
// ill-formed subpart. Total: there is no input this rejects.
//
// AFTER §STORAGE THIS STOPS BEING A DECODER AND BECOMES A SANITIZER, and the
// distinction is the reason it does not collapse into the core's lenient wrap.
// §Validity is Go-lean: a string is bytes that are *usually* UTF-8, so the core
// will happily wrap ill-formed bytes and let them read as U+FFFD only at the
// moment something iterates them. That wrap KEEPS THE ORIGINAL BYTES — `s.bytes()`
// hands them back unchanged, and `s.length` counts them. This function REWRITES
// them: what comes out is guaranteed well-formed UTF-8, whose bytes are EF BF BD
// where the bad bytes were, and the original is gone. Go draws the same line
// between `string(b)` and `strings.ToValidUTF8`. A caller who wants "show me
// something" wants the wrap; a caller who wants "give me bytes I can hand onward
// as text" wants this.
export function decodeUtf8Lossy(self: u8[]) {
  const r = decodeCore(self, 0, self.length, true)
  // Unreachable: the lossy path substitutes rather than returning the error arm.
  // The narrowing is what makes the `string` return type provable, and the trap
  // is what makes a future edit that breaks the invariant loud instead of
  // silent — VL has no way to say "this arm cannot happen" in a type.
  if r is Utf8Error {
    __trap__("std:utf8: lossy decode reported an error")
    return ""
  }
  r
}
